## Introduction
Systematic design and utilization of sustainable energy production technologies have gained enormous attention in recent years considering the continuously rising global energy demands and enormously diminishing natural energy resources [1-4]. As a matter of fact, electrocatalytic water splitting is greatly appreciated for its sustainable hydrogen production with extremely easy set-up and handling ability [5-7]. Particularly, water electrolysis involves the decomposition of water into hydrogen and oxygen gases by means of hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER), respectively. Despite that, the unfavorable reaction kinetics involved in OER process endorsed to be the bottle-neck in deciding the overall efficiency of water splitting [8-ll]. Thankfully, the precious metal-based oxides including $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ demonstrated noteworthy performance towards OER by diminishing the thermodynamic barrier. However, their limited reserves, unaffordable cost, and poor longrange maintainability in harsh alkaline environment make them inappropriate to be considered for large-scale usage. As a result, it is highly indispensable to design non-precious alternative electrocatalysts with significantly competitive performances and durability in affordable price.
Considering the significance of water electrolysis, wide variety of cost-effective transition metal-based electrocatalysts have been developed and noticed substantially improved performances with excellent long-term operation abilities [l2-18]. Particularly, due to the diversified topologies, large surface areas, adjustable porous nature, and tenability to introduce multi-metals, a vast number of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and their composites used as electrocatalysts toward water electrolysis [19-22].Interestingly, the OER activity of a pristine MOF is enormously enhanced by introducing the second or third metal into the MOF construction. Additional metal incorporation basically adjusts the intrinsic electrical conductivity of a material and modulates its surface to expose vast number of active sites for improved performance [23]. In particular, Ni/Fe-, Co/Fe-, and Ni/Co- combinations are widely studied in water electrolysis, owing to their vast terrestrial abundance, facile and low-cost fabrication methods, and enhanced structural and mechanical stability [24]. Recent literature studies approved the crucial surface electronic states modification in Nibased electrocatalysts $\mathrm{\DeltaNi^{2+}}$ to $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+/4+}$ ) by means of Fe-incorporation and that phenomenon significantly boosts the OER performance and also helps maintaining the overall composition of material during long-time operations [l3]. In addition, the importance of Fe and its effective synergism with Ni has also been established recently. Specifically, a small traces of Fe in $\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}$ proved to enhance the OER activity [25-27] and more importantly ${\mathrm{NiFeO}}_{x}{\mathrm{H}}_{y}$ is established as one of the best OER electrocatalysts reported to date [28].
But to our surprise, most of the established methods dealt with the incorporation of multi-metal sources during the material preparation stage, but integration of secondary metal by any other methods is hardly been established [12-14]. However, introducing the secondary metal sites precisely on the materials surface by systematic  surface engineering  strategy for a  particular functionality is scarcely been reported [15, 19, 29,30], and in most cases, the proposed methods have not been subjected to variety of precursor systems [20]. Besides, considering the deprived electrical conductivity, seldom mechanical, thermal, and chemical stability during long-time operations, MOFs are mainly used as sacrificial templates to obtain MOF-derived materials, and assimilated their water splitting abilities [22,31]. Moreover, the high temperature annealing often resulted in reduced specific surface area with demolished porous network and the bulk material formed by MOF-decomposition seize the active species, which make MOFderived  materials  incompatible for  water  electrolysis [32]. Fascinatingly, controlled annealing strategy of a MOF to accomplish active species within its architecture with combined advantages of MOFs and MOF-derived materials only by regulating the annealing temperature has gained much research significance in recent years [32-36]. Despite delivering the exceptional water splitting performances, this strategy is still in its embryonic stage.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Material fabrication approach
## 2.1.1 Fabrication of Fe-doped Ni-MOF-derivatives
Carbon nanotubes-interlinked Ni-based porous MOF nanosheets (Ni-MOF $@$ CNT）werefabricated followingour  earlier report [37,38], briefly from a reaction between $\mathrm{Ni(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ and l,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (l,4-BDC) in a mixed N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF)， ethanol $\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{5}O H)}$ ， and water solution. Now, $270~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ （ $\mathrm{~i~mmol})$ and $250~\mathrm{mg}$ of pristine Ni-MOF@CNT were taken in a quartz boat for controlled carbonization treatment and made sure that the Fe-source occupies the upstream side of $\Nu_{2}$ gas flow. Subsequently, the furnace temperature was raised to $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a rampage rate of $5~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ and maintained for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The as-obtained sample after annealing process was denoted as $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ ：Afterwards, in view of elucidating the annealing temperature effect, the carbonization was carried out at slightly higher temperatures, such as $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ 1 $\mathrm{\DeltaNi-M}\ @{\mathrm{C}}-500)$ and $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-600}^{\prime},$ keeping the reaction time and weights of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and Ni-MOF@CNT constant.
## 2.1.2 Fabrication of Fe-doped Cr, Mn, Co, and Cu based semiMOFs
Analogous $\mathrm{Cr}$ -MOF@CNT，Mn-MOF $@$ CNT,Co-MOF@CNT, and $\mathrm{c}_{\mathrm{u-MOF}@\mathrm{CNT}}$ samples have been prepared in the same manner as described for Ni-MOF $@$ CNT, except that the metal source was changed to $\mathrm{Cr(NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ ， $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}.$ and $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ respectively. Subsequently, $270~\mathrm{\mg}$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1 mmol) and $250~\mathrm{\mg}$ of pristine $\mathrm{MOF@CNT}$ were placed in a quartz boat and subjected to controlled decomposition under flowing $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas. The as-obtained annealed derivatives have been named as $\mathrm{Cr-M@C-400}$ 。Mn$\scriptstyle\mathrm{M@C-400}$ Co-M@C-400, and $\mathrm{Cu-M@C-400}$
## 2.2 Materials characterization
## 2.2.1 Materials-structure and morphology evaluation
Crystalline patterns and the phase purity of as-prepared MOFs and MOF-derived materials have been elucidated by X-ray diffraction (XRD) study (Rigaku D/MAX-rA diffractometer with Cu Kα radiation source). Chemical composition and their oxidation states in $\mathrm{Ni-M}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}400$ and $\mathrm{Co-M@C-400}$ have been realized by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis (Kratos XSAM800 with monochromatic Al-Kα source). Raman analysis of Ni-MOF, Co-MOF, and their derivatives have been realized by confocal Raman microscope (Renishaw Corporation, excitation wavelength was $532~\mathrm{nm}$ . The thermal decomposition pathway of pristine MOF (Ni-MOF@CNT) was investigated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, SDT Q600). Morphologies of various MOFs and corresponding derivatives were evidenced by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM, FEI Quanta 650 SEM)， while the Ni-M@C-400 (semi-MOF) nanostructure and the corresponding lattice fringes have been manifestedfrom high-resolutiontransmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM, Titan G260-300). Similarly, the available surface area and the porous nature of Ni-M@C-400 were estimated by $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms (BrunauerEmmett-Teller theory, Quantachrome, Nova 2000e).
## 2.2.2 Water oxidation performance evaluation
Water electrolysis performance of all the new samples prepared has been  investigated in $1\ \mathrm{~M~}$ potassium hydroxide (KOH) solution using a standard three-electrode set-up assembled by catalysts modified glassy carbon electrode (GCE, working electrode), counter electrode (graphite rod), and a reference electrode $\mathrm{(Hg/HgO}$ electrode)utilizing the CHI 660D electrochemical work station. First of all, $4~\mathrm{mg}$ of as-prepared sample was dispersed in $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of distilled water and ethanol mixture (3:1 of $\mathrm{{v}/\mathrm{{v}}}$ ）by ultrasonication for $5~\mathrm{min}$ 。followed by addition of $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of Nafion ink and then ultrasonication was continued for additional $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . As-obtained catalysts ink was drop-casted on polished GC electrodes (diameter $=0.003\mathrm{~m~}$ and catalyst loading $=0.28~\mathrm{mg/cm^{2}}.$ ）and dried in an electric oven for $10~\mathrm{min}$ prior to water electrolysis test. These catalysts loaded GC electrodes have been subjected to cyclic voltammetry tests ( $20\mathrm{CV}$ cycles) in order to activate the material before being realized the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves. The LSV tests have been carried out at $5\mathrm{mV}/s$ scan rate in view to evaluating the overpotentials at different current densities $(j)$ and afterwards, the obtained LSV plots have been used to assess the corresponding Tafel slopes in view of investigating the reaction kinetics. Particularly, the potential values obtained using $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ reference electrode were calibrated versus reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) using the equation of $E_{\mathrm{RHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Hg/HgO}}+0.923\ \mathrm{V}$ and the overpotentials presented in this study were estimated from the LSV plots using the equation of $E_{\mathrm{RHE}}\mathrm{~-~}1.23\mathrm{~V}.$ ，Besides, the accessible electrochemical active surface area (ECSA） of asprepared electrocatalysts was evaluated by conducting cyclic voltammograms within a non-Faradic current region. While the charge-transfer capabilities and the electrode/electrolyte interface resistance were investigated by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS, frequency range $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 100\$ kHz to $0.1~\mathrm{\Hz})$ · Moreover, the CV cycling stability was assessed by 2,000 continuous CV cycles on GC electrode, while the long-time i-t (chronoamperometry test) was performed on $\mathrm{Ni}$ foam current collector ( ${1}\mathrm{cm}\times2\mathrm{cm}$ catalyst loading was $2\mathrm{mg/cm^{2}}.$ 。
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Systematic fabrication and evaluation of Ni/Fe-based semi-MOFs
Electrochemically active nanoparticles implanted semi-MOFs have been fabricated by a unique vapor phased iron-doping strategy into a series of earth-abundant transition metal-based pristine MOFs. At first, Ni-MOF and its CNT-composite have been fabricated following our earlier report and the XRD patterns approved the MIL-type of MOF coordination (Fig.Sl(a) in the ESM)[37]. Initially, the Ni-MOF@CNT was subjected to TGA analysis in order to figure out its thermal behavior. According to Fig. l(a), a gradual loss in weight was noticed until $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ （ $\sim5\%$ weight loss) that corresponds to the removal of adsorbed water and the solvent molecules, while the actual MOF-decomposition begins at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and the maximum organic unit's decomposition can be realized at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Therefore, based on the TGA data, the Ni-MOF@CNT was first subjected to partial decomposition at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and as anticipated Ni nanoparticles implanted semi-MOF (Ni@semi-MOF) was obtained (Fig. S1(b) in the ESM). In general, MOFs upon high temperature annealing produce metal nanoparticles embedded porous carbon, as this Ni-MOF@CNT was carbonized at a controlled annealing temperature, the asobtained material demonstrated Ni nanoparticles within partially decomposed MOF. Recent pioneering research efforts approved that the sublimated heteroatoms such as S, Se, and P can be introduced into the MOF during carbonization leading to the successful isolation of metal chalcogenides and/or phosphides [5,39,40]. While the sublimated heterometal-doping during carbonization is scarcely been reported. To be specific, Sun et al. established the effective incorporation of sublimated Fe into the zeolitic imidazole framework-8 (ZIF-8） derived porous carbon [41],and Liang et al. demonstrated the successful utilization of sublimated Fe in fabricating the heterogeneous $\mathrm{CoFe_{2}O_{4}/C o O_{\it x}}$ electrocatalysts [42].
On the basis of above experimental results and the discussion, $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has been considered as an appropriate Fe-source owing to its low sublimation point $(280-285~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , and placed this iron-source at the upstream position of $\Nu_{2}$ gas flow in addition to the Ni-MOF $@$ CNT and subjected to controlled annealing temperature at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [32,43].For the as-obtained $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ material, XRD patterns disclosed the presence of remnant MOF peak at $9.2^{\circ}$ and the co-existence of $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ (JCPDS No. 87- 2336) and Ni (JCPDS No. 01-1258) heteronanoparticles within the CNT-network (Scheme 1 and Fig. 1(b)). While, the XRD patterns of MOF annealed at higher temperature $\left(\mathrm{Ni-M}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{C}-500\right)$ under Fevapor approved the complete MOF-decomposition and displayed $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ and Ni heteronanoparticles within the CNT-network (Fig. S2(a) in the ESM). Surprisingly, further increase in annealing temperature to $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(\mathrm{Ni-M}\ @\mathrm{C-}600),$ produced the unique NiFe alloy composition within CNT-network (Fig.S2(b) in the ESM). Aforementioned TGA and XRD investigations indicate that the $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ annealing under Fe-vapor only can yield the heteronanoparticles embedded semi-MOF. Besides that, the bimetallic NiFe-MOF@CNT carbonized under the same set of conditions( $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h};}$ NiFe-M@C-400; without additional Fesource) demonstrated the significant decrease in MOF peak (100) crystal plane of MOF at $9.2^{\circ})$ ，which approves the maximum decomposition of MOF-skeleton, despite approving the presence of $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ (JCPDS No. 87-2336), and Ni (JCPDS No. 01-1258) combination in its XRD analysis (Fig. 1(b)). This further approves the significance of sublimated Fe-doping strategy in fabricating the active nanoparticles implanted semi-MOFs.
Besides, in order to realize the universality of vapor phased Fedoping strategy, Co-MOF@CNT has been subjected to controlled annealing under vaporized Fe-source $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-M@C-400}$ ）and to our surprise, it yields only ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanoparticles within semi-MOF composite (Fig.S2(c) in the ESM). Evidently, the bimetallic CoFe-MOF $@$ CNT upon controlled annealing (CoFe-M@C-400) yielded the cobalt ferrite nanoparticles within extensively decomposed MOF. Besides, high temperature annealing $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{Co-M@C-500})$ ）produced the combination of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ heteronanoparticles within CNT-matrix similar to $\mathrm{Ni-M}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}500$ (Fig. S2(d) in the ESM) [43]. Subsequently, this new strategy has been extended to $\mathrm{Cr-MOF@CNT}$ 。 $\mathrm{Mn-MOF@CNT}$ 。and CuMOF@CNT. Wherein, Cr- and Mn- MOFs produced the corresponding ferrite nanoparticles $\mathrm{(MFe_{2}O_{4};}$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Cr}$ or Mn) within partially decomposed MOF [44], while the CuMOF@CNT delivered the combination of $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.86}\mathrm{Fe}_{2.14}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and Cu heteronanoparticles within the remnant MOF (Fig.S3 in the ESM) [45]. Moreover, due to the high crystalline nature of metalconstituents, the carbon nanotubes diffraction peaks are rather invisible in aforementioned samples, but are unambiguously determined by the characteristic D and G bands of CNT in their Raman spectral analysis (Fig. 1(c), and Figs. S4(a) and S4(b) in the ESM) [46-48]. Figure 1(c) presents the data of Raman spectra of pristine Ni-MOF and its corresponding annealed derivative (Ni$\mathrm{M@C-400}$ ） in comparison with extensively decomposed bimetallic MOF (NiFe-M@C-400). Precisely, the characteristic Raman bands of Ni-MOF (related to 1,4-BDC) unambiguously detected in Ni$\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{C}{-}400$ at 1,615 and $1{,}440\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ $(-\mathrm{COO}_{\mathrm{stretching}})$ ， $1,140~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{C-O}_{\mathrm{bending}})$ ，and 865 and $630~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ $(\mathrm{C-H}_{\mathrm{stretching}})$ [14,49], confirming the semi-MOF composition even after annealing treatment, while the same are almost disappeared in NiFe-M@C400 as it undergoes maximum decomposition.
In addition, the formation of $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ has been evidenced by the existence of $\mathrm{T}_{2\mathrm{g}}^{3}$ 0 $\sim315\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ and $\mathrm{A_{1g}}$ （ $\sim462$ and $675\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ peaksin both1 $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Ni-M@C-400}}$ and NiFe-M@C-400 samples (Fig.1(c)）[50]． Besides, $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-500}$ also displayed the characteristic Raman bands of nickel ferrite at $\sim203\ c m^{-1}(\mathrm{T}_{2\mathrm{g}}^{1}).$ $322\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\left(\mathrm{T}_{2\mathrm{g}}^{3}\right)$ 。 $567~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}(\mathrm{T}_{2\mathrm{g}}^{2})$ 。and $674\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\mathrm{(A_{1g})}$ 。as it also possessed the $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}/N i}$ heteronanoparticles composition. Evidently, pristine MOF related bands are disappeared in both Ni$\mathrm{M}\ @\mathrm{C}-500$ and $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-600}$ as they underwent complete decomposition (Fig.S4(a) in the ESM). In the same way, Co$\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{C}{-}400$ and the bimetallic( $\mathsf{\Delta}_{\mathrm{coFe-M@C-400}}$ displayed  the characteristic Raman bands correspond to ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ at $312~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\left(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{g}}\right)$ $460\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ $\left(\mathrm{T}_{2\mathrm{g}}\right)$ and $672\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\left(\mathrm{A}_{1g}\right)$ as both of them yielded the same composition but the former evidenced the additional peaks at 860, 1,132, 1,423, and $1{,}628\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to Co-MOF in approving its partial decomposition (Fig. S4(b) in the ESM) [5l]. All these experimental results revealed the fact that $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ annealing is favorable for semi-MOFs fabrication, and the aforementioned results collectively summarized that the newly established Fe-doping strategy can be successfully implemented to variety of MOF-systems in order to fabricate the surface modified electrocatalysts.
Additionally, the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherm of Ni$\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{C}{-}400$ displayed considerably high specific surface area of $56.95~\mathrm{\m^{2}/g}$ owing to its partially decomposed MOF-skeleton. Notably, very high pore diameter of $20.95~\mathrm{nm}$ has been detected for $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ (Fig.S4(c) in the ESM),which can allow the electrolyte penetration through the material for enhanced water electrolysis. Moreover, small hysteresis curve noticed within 0.4 to 0.6 relative pressures $\left(P/P_{0}\right)$ range signifying the presence of beneficial micro- and meso-pores, and the high $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake between 0.8 to $1.0\:P/P_{0}$ range suggests the possible meso- and macro-pores in Ni-M $@{\mathrm{C}}^{}{-}400$ (Fig. 1(d)) [52].
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) approves the ultrathin porous two-dimensional (2D) nanosheet morphology of Ni-MOF and upon CNT-integration (Ni-MOF@CNT), the porous sheetlike morphology transformed to CNT-interconnected individual porous nanosheets (Figs. 2(a) and 2(b), and Figs. S5(a)-S5(c) in the ESM) and almost the same morphology noticed for NiFe-MOF@CNTwithslightlyexposedCNT-network (Fig.S5(d) in the ESM). Besides, SEM analysis of Ni-M@C-400 clearly demonstrated the partially crumbled MOF-nanosheets morphology with interlinked CNT-network fastened by active $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}/N i}$ heteronanoparticles (Fig.2(c),and Figs. S6(a) and S6(b) in the ESM), similar morphology transformation was noticed for NiFe-M@C-400,wherein MOF-nanosheets have been mostly converted to nanoparticles and have interlinked by CNTnetwork and few broken MOF-nanosheets can be clearly visible in supporting the semi-MOF composition (Fig.S7 in the ESM). While,MOF-nanosheetscompletedecomposition  into $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}/N i}$ heterostructure fastened by CNT-network has been clearly evidenced in $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-500}$ (Figs. S6(c) and S6(d) in the ESM),and interestingly, large sized NiFe-alloy particles (more than $100\mathrm{nm}$ ）connected by CNT-matrix was detected in the high temperature annealed sample under Fe-vapor $\left(\mathrm{Ni-M@C-600}\right)$ by complete decomposition of MOF-sheets (Figs. S6(e) and S6(f) in the ESM). Contrarily, porous sheets accumulated rectangular blocks in CNT-mesh morphology was detected for CoMOF@CNT and was transformed to compressed nanoplates-like CNT-composite upon controlled carbonization under Fe-vapor 0 $\mathrm{Co-M@C-400}$ ：Fig.S8 in the ESM). Additionally, the unique porous MOF-nanosheets interlinked by CNT-framework structure has been clearly detected in TEM analysis of ${\mathrm{Ni-M}}{\mathcal{Q}}{\mathrm{C}}-$ 400 (Fig.2(d) and Fig.S9 in the ESM). Basically, pristine MOFs upon high temperature annealing under inert atmosphere produce corresponding metal nanoparticles embedded carbon matrix, and as we have systematically decomposed the Ni-MOF into its semi-MOF, Ni nanoparticles have been derived from the corresponding nanosheets [38]. As it was established earlier, the newly implanted nanoparticles can either be located on the surface of material [33,35,36] or distributed on the surface and/or inner part of the MOF-nanostructure [32]. In particular, the randomly distributed bright spots represent the nanosheets-derived Ni nanoparticles (Fig.2(c) and Fig. S9 in the ESM).
Subsequently, the Ni-source presented on the surface (Ni nanoparticles and the Ni-O bonds of crumbled MOF) transformed to $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ in the presence of sublimated Fe during carbonization. As a result, the surface mostly occupied by $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ nanoparticles which are confirmed by the (3ll) crystal plane detected in HR-TEM images (brown circles in Fig.2(f) with $0.252\quad\mathrm{nm}$ interplanar spacing). While, the lattice fringes correspond to bright spots (Ni nanoparticles) are overshadowed due to the surface nickel ferrite formation, however, they can be unambiguously determined in various locations ((lll） crystal plane with $0.204~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ interplanar distance; green circles in Fig.2(f)). Precisely, the growth of Ni nanoparticles and the Fedoping take place at the same time, hence the surface of Ni-M@C400 mostly occupied by $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ and the uniform distribution of Fe on materials surface can be realized from its high-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) and energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) data (Figs. $2(\mathrm{g}){-}2(\mathrm{k})$ and Fig.S10 in the ESM). In particular, the EDS-elemental mapping data approved that the bright spots in TEM images are the MOFderived Ni nanoparticles (Fig.2(h)) and also confirm the iron uniform distribution in the material (Fig.2(i)). Similar such Felayered NiCo alloy within a semi-MOF was fabricated by Li et al. from a pristine NiCoFe-MOF by a controlled decomposition strategy. In which, the NiCo alloy formation (via Ni-O and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-O}$ bond breakage) takes place much faster than the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ owing to the low Ni-O and $_{\mathrm{Co-O}}$ bond energy, and hence the lately formed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (via Fe-O bond breakage) occupies the surface of NiCo alloy. Density functional theory (DFT） calculations also approved that this type of hetero composition enhances the OER performance by means of exchange stabilization phenomenon of active oxygen units [32].
Moreover, the existing elemental composition and their corresponding oxidation states on the surface of $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ have been elucidated by XPS measurement. As anticipated, this Fedoped semi-MOF confirmed the co-existence of Ni, Fe, O, and C elements on its surface (Fig.3(b)). Moreover, the Ni 2p XPS spectra displayed two main peaks at $856.42\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\eV}}$ $\mathrm{(Ni~}2\ensuremath{\mathbf{p}}_{3/2}\mathrm{)}$ and $874.02\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Ni $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}\mathrm{,}$ correspond to $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ oxidation state together with associated satellite peaks at 86l.91 and $880.28\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 3(c)) [53]. The peak positions approved the negative shift in binding energies $\mathrm{(\sim0.44~eV)}$ for Ni $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{p}}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ compared to its pristine MOF,which confirms the modified electronic states of Ni after carbonization, as it develops nickel ferrite nanoparticles within its architecture [38, 54].Although,the existence of $\mathrm{Ni^{\mathrm{\circ}}}$ in Ni$\textcircled{\scriptsize{1}@\mathrm{C}{-}400}$ was unambiguously detected by XRD and EDSmapping analysis, the corresponding peak appeared to be almost invisible $(853.8\mathrm{eV})$ in its surface analysis, confirming that most of the $\mathrm{Ni^{\circ}}$ internally occupied in the material compared to the materials surface. This theory was well supported by the Ni:Fe metal ratio detected by XPS survey on $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ surface (1:1.2) and the internal metal ratio detected by EDS elemental analysis (1:0.5) (Fig. 3(a)).
In addition, the Fe 2p XPS spectra displayed two main peaks with corresponding satellite peaks, in which the peaks positioned at $710.58\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fe $\mathrm{2p}_{3/2}\mathrm{,}$ ）and $723.63\mathrm{~eV}$ (Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2})$ represent the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ oxidation state of iron, and the other two peaks positioned at 712.61 eV(Fe $\mathrm{2p}_{3/2}\mathrm{,}$ ）and $724.8~\mathrm{eV}$ (Fe $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}\mathsf{)}$ correspond to the $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ oxidation state of iron (Fig.3(d)) [53]. In particular, the peak positions of Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ are considerably lowered $(\sim\ 0.42$ to $1.17\mathrm{eV},$ ）compared to the reported ${\mathrm{NiFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ -based materials [22, 53, 55],which means the Femetal center possesses high electron density in its 3d-orbitals and thereby converts into Fe-O(OH) state during water oxidation [13, 37]. The change in electron density and the shift in peak position for both $\mathrm{Ni}\ 2\mathrm{p}$ and Fe 2p XPS spectra, approved the significant electronic interactions between two metals [53]. Moreover,the presence of $\mathrm{Ni^{\circ}}$ 。 $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ 。 $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}$ oxidation states of elements in $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ approved the presence of $\mathrm{Ni/Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ combination.
Similarly, XPS spectral analysis of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-M@C-400}$ approved the co-existence of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Co},}\end{array}$ Fe, O, and C elements as expected. The corelevel $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathrm{p}$ spectra displayed two main peaks correspond to $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Co}}$ $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ with associated satellite peaks (787.24 and 803.87 eV). The main peaks further deconvoluted into two independent peaks correspond to $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ (781.58 and $797.52\ \mathrm{eV})$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ (783.31 and $799.43\mathrm{eV},$ ）oxidation states of cobalt (Fig.3(e)) [56]. While, the Fe 2p spectrum deconvoluted into two main peaks correspond to Fe $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ (710.73 and $713.49\mathrm{eV}$ and Fe $2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ (723.4 and $724.82\ \mathrm{eV},$ ）with associated satellite peaks (787.24 and $803.87~\mathrm{eV}$ ：Fig.3(f). The peak positions and the oxidation states of Co ( $\mathrm{Co^{3+}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}},$ ）andFe( ${\mathrm{Fe}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe^{3+}}^{.}$ ）elements in corresponding XPS spectral analysis are well matched with the XPS data reported for $\mathrm{CoFe_{2}O_{4}}$ -based materials, in supporting the existence of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ in $\mathrm{Co-M@C-400}$ [56,57]. Besides, the oxygen ls XPS spectra of both the samples displayed two main peaks corresponding to metal-oxygen bonding peak (M-O; $\sim532.17\mathrm{eV})$ attributed to the $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ in $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400~and/o}$ r ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ in $\mathrm{Co-M@C-400}$ or M-O bond of semi-MOF along with the other metal-hydroxyl bond peak $\mathrm{(M\mathrm{-}O H;~\sim~533.66~\ e V)}$ attributed to the surface adsorbed water and/or the hydroxyl groups related to partially decomposed MOF-architecture (Figs. Sll(a) and S1l(c) in the ESM) [15]. Interestingly, the C 1s XPS spectra displayed mainly three peaks corresponding to semi-MOF related non-oxygenated C-C bonding peak $(\sim~284.78~\mathrm{eV})$ ， metal-oxygen unit (M-O-C at $\sim285.65\mathrm{eV}.$ ), and the carboxylate unit ( $scriptstyle\mathrm{{\overbrace{O=C-O}}}$ at $\sim289.09\ \mathrm{eV}$ ）(Figs. Sll(b) and Sil(d) in the ESM)[5]. In particular, the O ls and C 1s XPS spectral data emphasized the presence of partially decomposed MOF in both Ni$\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{C}{-}400$ and Co-M@C-400.
## 3.2 Water electrolysis performance evaluation
## 3.2.1 Oxygen evolution abilities
At first, Ni-MOF and its CNT-composite have been tested for OER activity and noticed the significance of CNT in improving the performance. Subsequently, the annealed samples of Ni$\mathrm{MOF@CNT}$ in the presence of Fe-vapor were scrutinized to elucidate the OER activities. Among the Fe-doped samples, the one prepared at relatively low annealing temperature (Ni-M@C400) displayed enormously improved OER activity with very low overpotential of $229\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve the benchmark current density 0 $\mathrm{{.10}\ m A/c m^{2}}$ 中e $\eta_{10})$ (Fig.S12(a) in the ESM). The $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ OER performance is extremely low compared to the commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalyst( $266\mathrm{mV}$ at $\eta_{10}\mathrm{,}$ , and the samples prepared at higher annealing temperatures ( $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-500}$ . $299~\mathrm{mV}$ at $\eta_{10}$ and Ni$\mathrm{M}@\mathrm{C}{\cdot}600\colon326\mathrm{mV}$ t $\eta_{10}^{\mathrm{~~}}.$ ). The improved OER performance of Ni$\textcircled{\scriptsize{1}@\mathrm{C}{-}400}$ clearly emphasized the importance of partially crumbled nanosheets with interconnected CNT fastened by active heteronanoparticles over the fully decomposed materials [32,33,38, 58].While, the improved OER activity of Ni$\textcircled{\scriptsize{1}@\mathrm{C}{-}400}$ and $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-500}$ over the $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-600}$ sample suggests the fact that the combination of heteronanoparticles $\mathrm{(Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ and Ni) is beneficial for OER performance over the sample with NiFe alloy particles alone in CNT-matrix (Fig. S12(a) in the ESM) [32]. Besides, the sample prepared without Fe-source (Ni@semi-MOF) displayed inferior OER performance ( $377\mathrm{mV}$ at $\eta_{10})$ compared to ${\mathrm{Ni-M}}\ @{\mathrm{C-400}}.$ that signifies the importance of multi-metal combination for enhanced OER activity [13].
Moreover, the recent theoretical and experimental investigations approved that the heterometal-doping can modulate the electronic structure, electrical conductivity, number of active sites, and intrinsic characteristics of a material in order to reduce the free energy barrier of reaction intermediates and thereby significantly boost the OER activity [34,4l,59]. Similarly, the annealed Ni-MOF without the integration of CNT (Ni-MOF-400) required a high overpotential of $383~\mathrm{mV}$ $(\eta_{10})$ which is $154~\mathrm{mV}$ high overpotential compared to the sample prepared with CNT 1 $\scriptstyle\mathrm{\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}}$ ）that highlights the significance of interconnected CNT-network [60]. Altogether, the overpotential detected for Ni$\textcircled{\scriptsize{1}@\mathrm{C}{-}400}$ is one of the best OER performances detected so far on GCE including the pristine MOFs and MOF-derived composites (Table Sl in the ESM).
Interestingly, the OER performance of $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ is much better than the conventional bimetallic MOF-derived NiFe-M@C400 ( $270\mathrm{mV}$ at $\eta_{10}\mathrm{,}$ prepared under the same set of experimental conditions, which demonstrates the significance of vapor phased Fe-doing strategy over the traditional multi  metal-doping (Fig.4(a)). Similarly, $\mathrm{Co-M@C-400}$ also displayed considerably improved OER activity ( $254~\mathrm{mV}$ at $\eta_{10})$ over its bimetallic MOFderivative (CoFe-M@C-400; $288~\mathrm{mV}$ at $\eta_{10})$ in supporting the vaporized  Fe-doping.This aforementioned comparative investigation suggests that the maximum decomposition taking place in bimetallic MOFs is not favorable for the OER application as most of the active sites possibly blocked by the bulk graphitic carbon produced during carbonization [32,58]. While the controlled decomposition taking place in monometallic MOFs under Fe-vapor expected to yield active species in partially crumbled nanosheets and thereby displayed much better OER activity. Moreover, the high OER performances of $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ and $_{\mathrm{NiFe-M@C-400}}$ over the Co/Fe-based catalysts suggest the significance of heteronanoparticles combination $\mathrm{(Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}/N i)}$ over the existence of ${\mathrm{CoFe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ nanoparticles alone in CNT. Besides, among the other semi-MOFs, $\mathrm{Cu-M@C-400}$ 0 $297\mathrm{mV}$ at $\eta_{10})$ exhibited considerably better OER activity over the other electrocatalysts, $\mathrm{Cr-M@C-400}$ （ $342~\mathrm{mV}$ at $\eta_{10})$ and $\mathrm{Mn-M}\ @\mathrm{C}\cdot400$ $(327~\mathrm{~mV}$ at $\eta_{10})$ ， possibly owing to the heteronanoparticles synergism.
In addition to the superior OER activity, $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ also displayed very low Tafel slope (40.5l mV/dec) by keeping its superiority in overwhelming the thermodynamic barrier. Although the other Fe-doped samples fabricated at higher annealing temperature require large overpotentials, they do exhibit feasible OER kinetics (Ni-M@C-500: 43.97 mV/dec and Ni ${\mathrm{.}}{\mathrm{M@C}}.$ 60 $)0\colon47.05\mathrm{mV}/\mathrm{dec},$ signifying the superiority of Ni/Fe-based OER catalysts, over the benchmark $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ catalyst $\langle52.56\ \mathrm{\mV/dec}\rangle$ Although, the Tafel slopes of CoFe-based semi-MOFs are slightly higher than the Ni/Fe-based semi-MOFs, they are significantly better than state-of-the-art $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ electrocatalyst (Fig. 4(b)). To our surprise, the other semi-MOFs including Cr-M@C-400, Mn-M@C400, and $\mathrm{Cu-M@C-400}$ displayed surprisingly higher Tafel slopes despite showing appreciable OER activity (Fig.Sl3 in the ESM), which demonstrates the feasibility of Ni/Fe- and Co/Fecombinations in lowering the thermodynamic barrier for OER [60,61].
Moreover, the estimated electrochemical surface area from cyclic voltammograms operated within non-Faradic capacitive current range approved the enlarged $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ values for $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ $(5.63\mathrm{mF}/\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-M@C-400}$ $(3.17\:\:\mathrm{\mF/cm^{2}}$ ）over the correspondingbimetalliccounterparts(NiFe-M@C-400: $2.36~\mathrm{mF/cm^{2}}$ and $\mathrm{CoFe-M@C-400:1.73~mF/cm^{2}})$ , which suggests the availability of high electrochemically accessible surface area and also confirms the highest possibility of active sites exposure at the electrode/electrolyte interface for the improved OER activity (Fig.4(c) and Fig.Sl4 in the ESM). In addition, a very low charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ was observed for the semi-MOF derivatives, including $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ 1 $\left(8.23\Omega\right)$ ，NiFe-M@C-400 (10.63 $\Omega_{\prime}^{\prime}$ ， $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-M@C-400}$ $\left(10.30\quad\Omega\right)$ ，and ${\mathrm{CoFe-M@C-400}}$ $\left(11.77\Omega\right)$ in electrochemical impedance spectroscopy measured at a fixed $229\mathrm{mV}$ overpotential (Fig.4(d)), among which ${\mathrm{Ni-M}}{\mathcal{Q}}{\mathrm{C}}-$ 400 displayed the smallest value of $R_{\mathrm{ct}}.$ Overall, $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ established to be one of the best OER electrocatalysts reported to date owing to its partial MOF-features, heteronanoparticles synergism, mechanical stability, and electrical conductivity enhancement of CNT-network and largely exposed active catalytic sites with smallest charge transfer resistance. Besides, ${\mathrm{Ni-M}}{\mathcal{Q}}C-$ 400/NF also displayed exceptional durability in a $60\mathrm{~h~}i_{-}t$ test conducted at a current density of $30\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and the CV plots obtained before and after the i-t test are almost identical (Figs. 4(e) and 4(f)).
## 3.2.2 Post-OER investigations
In general, transition metal-based electrocatalysts undergo in situ surface oxidation during OER process and the modified surface is considered as the actual catalytic site for OER activity [62, 63].As can be seen in Fig. 4(a), the small redox peak appeared $(\sim1.38\:\mathrm{V})$ prior to the OER onset potential for both $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ and NiFe$\scriptstyle\mathrm{M@C-400}$ signify that the surface of those materials is getting converted to oxidized phase and is served as the actual catalytic site for OER process [64]. Moreover, in most Ni/Fe-based electrocatalysts, Ni is considered as the active species for OER application and the Fe-source expected to adjust the surface electronic states of Ni $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ to $\mathrm{Ni^{3+/4+}}$ ） during redox process in order to enhance the OER activity [25,65]. And in most cases, the OER proceeds via Ni-O(OH) and/or Fe-O(OH) layer formation [54,66], while the complete transformation of a material during water oxidation is scarcely noticed [15,49,67]. Therefore, in order to elucidate the real catalytic sites in $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-}$ 400, the sample after 2,ooo CV cycles has been analyzed. Despite delivering almost the same LSV plot after $2{,}000\mathrm{CV}$ cycles (Fig. 5(a)), the material transformed its morphology into porous mesh-like CNT-framework with randomly exposed bright Ni nanoparticles (Fig. S15 in the ESM). In addition, no clear evidence of any other crystalline phases was detected in its XRD analysis after OER cycling and approved the semi-MOF composition with $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ and Ni heteronanoparticles, suggesting the robust structural stability (Fig. 5(b)).
Moreover, captivating binding energy dislocation has been detected in Fe 2p XPS spectra of Ni-M $@(-400$ after OER, wherein the peaks correspond to $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ shifted to ( $_{\sim1.18}$ to $2.28\:\mathrm{eV},$ higher binding energy, confirming the elevated 3d-electron density in Fe towards forming the oxyhydroxide (Fe-O(OH)) intermediate in order to facilitate the OER catalysis (Fig.5(c)) [65,68]. Additionally, the metal-oxygen peak in O ls spectra slightly moved to lower binding energy $(0.25\mathrm{eV})$ in approving the new metal-O(OH) formation (Fig.Sl6(b） in the ESM) [65], which means a slight portion of Fe-doped surface $\left(\mathrm{Ni}_{0.4}\mathrm{Fe}_{2.6}\mathrm{O}_{4}\right)$ transformed to Fe-O(OH) layer during water oxidation and the internal composition is same as before, this hypothesis was also supported by the new broad peak correspond to Fe-O(OH) phase appeared at $505{-}620~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in its Raman analysis (Fig.5(d)) [40,69]. In general, the Raman peaks correspond to $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ 0 $322\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{T}_{2\mathrm{g}}^{3})$ ， $567~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}(\mathrm{T}_{~2\mathrm{g}}^{2}).$ and $674~\mathrm{cm^{-1}(A_{1g})})$ appeared in this region and the intensified peak appearance after OER suggests that the surface occupied nickel ferrite material is basically transformed to Fe-O(OH) layer during OER cycles and the internal composition is unaffected [70,71]. Therefore, based on the post-OER characterizations, we believe that the surface occupied $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ material mainly transformed to Fe-O(OH) layer during OER catalysis and the synergistic interactions between Fe-O(OH) layer and the unaffected internal composition are the actual reaction sites for OER application.
Considering the remarkable OER activity of $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ overall water splitting ability of this semi-MOF electrocatalyst has been evaluated by constructing a two-electrode system with commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (as cathode) [5,40,72]. As can be seen in Fig S17(c) in the ESM, significantly better full water splitting performance was noticed for $\mathrm{Pt/C@NF||Ni-M@C-400@NF}$ combination over commercial $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C@NF}||\mathrm{RuO}_{2}@\mathrm{NF}$ combination on nickel foam current collectors (loading of $2\ \mathrm{\mg/cm^{2}})$ particularly at higher current densities. In particular, a low cell voltage of $1.76\mathrm{~V~}$ is required for $\mathrm{Pt/C@NF||Ni-M@C-400@NF}$ combination to achieve the current density of $50~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2};$ while a high potential of $142~\mathrm{~mV}$ is needed for the commercial electrocatalysts combination to achieve the same current density (1.902 V at $\eta_{50})$ .Additionally, $\mathrm{Ni-M@C-400}$ loaded $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ foam substrates combination $\mathrm{(Ni-M}{\mathcal{Q}}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}400@\mathrm{NF}$ alone) also displayed noteworthy full water splitting performance ( $\mathrm{i}.95\mathrm{~V~}$ at $\eta_{50})$ but is significantly lower than the $\mathrm{Pt/C@NF||Ni-M@C-400@NF}$ combination owing to the inadequate HER activity of ${\mathrm{Ni-M}}{\mathcal{Q}}C-$ 400 ( $209\mathrm{mV}$ at $\eta_{10},$ Fig. S17(b) in the ESM). Besides that, Faraday efficiency (FE) of this $\mathrm{Pt/C@NF||Ni-M@C-400@NF}$ combination has been elucidated by collecting the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ gases released during overall water splitting catalysis. Specifically, $\sim15~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\sim7.5\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ gases have been collected by a lab-made water drainage system (Fig. S18 in the ESM)[ ESM) [73, 74] and the v/v of obtained $\mathrm{H}_{2}{:}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ represents the theoretical ratio of 2:l, confirming that the employed electric current is exclusively used for the water spitting catalysis and hence the estimated FE for both HER and OER is about $100\%$ (Fig. S17(d) in the ESM).
## 4 Conclusions
This study establishes the novel surface engineering strategy to transform the material surface into secondary-metal doped composition. As the secondary metal-doped at a regulated carbonization conditions, the obtained materials surface is different from its internal composition leading to the heterocomposition synergism. Owing to the combined structural features related to MOF-architecture, electrochemically active heteronanoparticles synergism, interconnected conductive CNTnetwork electrical and mechanical support, as-obtained semi-MOF systems displayed noteworthy OER activity. The post-OER characterizations revealed the fact that the surface occupied $\mathrm{Ni_{0.4}F e_{2.6}O_{4}}$ transformed to Fe-O(OH) layer during OER catalysis and its productive synergism with unaffected internal composition is the actual reason for improved OER. This work also demonstrates the versatility of sublimated Fe-doping strategy and among the variety of composites, Ni- and Co- semi-MOFs displayed their dominance over commercial $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ in terms of OER performance and lowering the thermodynamic barrier. The proposed generalized Fe-doping strategy can open up the path towards disclosing the universally acceptable approaches in order to design and fabricate the advanced electrocatalysts for water electrolysis.
